    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Error: Please provide exactly one argument.\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Get the argument from command line
    char *input = argv[1];

    // Print the original argument
    printf("Original Argument: %s\n", input);

    // Trim trailing whitespace
    int len = strlen(input);
    while (len > 0 && isspace((unsigned char)input[len - 1])) {
        input[--len] = 0;
    }

    // Print the argument after trimming whitespaces
    printf("Argument After Trim: %s\n", input);

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```
Please note that in C, strings are not automatically modified when passed to a function. So, this code modifies `input` directly. If you want to create a copy of the string and only trim it there, then you should use `strdup()` or similar functions to duplicate the string before modifying it.
