['Here is an example of how you could implement this in C using sockets for communication with HTTP protocol. For simplicity, let's assume we are communicating over localhost and port 8080. This code does not handle errors or edge cases that may occur during the communication process. It also assumes that the request from the client is a simple string containing only the username.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <unistd.h>

void http_hello_page(int request, int response) {
    char buffer[1024];  // Assume the username won't be longer than this
    memset(buffer, 0, sizeof(buffer));

    // Read from request file stream (i.e., the client socket)
    read(request, buffer, sizeof(buffer)-1);

    char response_message[256];
    snprintf(response_message, sizeof(response_message), "HTTP/1.0 200 OK\r\nContent-Length: %d\r\n\r\nHello %s", strlen("Hello ") + strlen(buffer) + 1, buffer);

    // Write to response file stream (i.e., the client socket)
    write(response, response_message, strlen(response_message));
}
```
In this example, we're not directly dealing with request and response streams as you specified in your problem, but instead handling HTTP requests over a TCP socket connection.

Please note that to run this code successfully, you need to have a running server which is set up to handle such connections appropriately.
