['Here's how you could implement this using Python decorators and Flask framework for web development. We will use Jinja2 templating engine to escape HTML special characters.

```python
from flask import Flask, request
from markupsafe import escape
app = Flask(__name__)
url_mapping = {}  # stores mapping between URLs and corresponding functions

def map_url(url):  
    def decorator(func):  
        url_mapping[url] = func  
        return func  
    return decorator

@map_url('/hello/<username>')  
def hello(username):  
    return "Hello, {}!".format(escape(username))

@app.route('/', defaults={'path': ''})
@app.route('/<path:path>')
def catch_all(path):
    for url, func in url_mapping.items():
        if request.path == url:
            return func(request.view_args['username'])  # assuming username is a route parameter
    return "Page not found", 404

if __name__ == '__main__':  
    app.run()
```
You can run this program and open your browser to visit the URL http://localhost:5000/hello/your_username to see a greeting message with your username. If the path does not match any of registered routes, it will return "Page not found" message. 

Note: This code is for demonstration purposes only and should be used as a starting point. It lacks error handling, logging etc. that are important in production-ready applications.
']

